Shell runtime architecture decisions

- No new permission mode, sandbox, environment filtering, or shell capability restriction was introduced. Existing permission and destructive-command policies remain unchanged.

- Main execution uses the inherited environment plus one bounded in-memory user-profile snapshot per agent session, then the user shell directly. No plaintext profile snapshot is written to disk.

- The old heuristic background launcher and default 120-second timeout were removed. Commands yield after 10 seconds by default and continue through bash_write, with optional stdin, polling, stopping, and explicit hard timeout.

- Every process gets a dedicated process group. Cancellation and application-exit cleanup stop the complete process tree on Unix and Windows.

- Pre/post workspace scans were replaced by a shared bounded event watcher. Cold watcher setup is isolated from the async runtime, capped at one second, runs in parallel with profile capture, and ignores generated dependency/build directories.

- Model and live previews are bounded. Full output storage is created lazily only above 28 KiB, uses private files, and is cleaned on failed or incomplete creation.

- Redundant detected and started diagnostic persistence was removed; final completion diagnostics remain. Root-session subagent activity checks are cached and bounded.

